# Commonly confused words: "then" in a comparison where "than" is meant.
# From common confused-word lists; examples are ours.
# why: Than compares (bigger than, rather than). Then is about time or order (first this, then that).
expr main [<([rather, sooner, more, less, fewer, better, worse, bigger, smaller, larger, higher, lower, longer, shorter, faster, slower, older, younger, easier, harder, cheaper, greater, other, further, farther, earlier, later, quicker, stronger, weaker, happier, worse, nicer, safer, richer, poorer, smarter, louder, quieter, warmer, colder, hotter, closer, simpler, wider, deeper, heavier, lighter, newer] then [I, me, you, he, she, we, they, him, her, us, them, it, the, a, an, my, your, his, our, their, this, that, ever, before, usual, expected, planned, last, anyone, anything, everyone, everything, most, many, any, one, two, three, ten, half, average, normal, necessary, needed, required, drive, walk, wait, pay, go, stay, buy, rent, work]), then>, <(rather [VERB] then), then>]

let message "In comparisons, use `than` (`more than`, `rather than`); `then` is about time or order."
let description "Fixes `then` in comparisons: `bigger then`, `rather walk then drive`."
let kind "Malapropism"
let becomes "than"
let strategy "MatchCase"

test "I'd rather walk then drive." "I'd rather walk than drive."
test "This is better then before." "This is better than before."
test "She is older then me." "She is older than me."
test "It took longer then expected." "It took longer than expected."


allows "We ate, and then we left."
allows "Finish the draft first, then send it."
allows "It got better, then worse."
allows "If it's later then that's fine."
